  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more left-skewed—i.e., one that assigns higher probability to its best outcome. In this case, Lottery A assigns 25% to 3000€, and Lottery B assigns 20% to 4000€. Since 25% > 20%, Lottery A is more left-skewed in terms of probability mass on the best outcome, and thus the reason would favor Lottery A. The individual considers the probabilities (20% vs 25%) and compares them meaningfully, noting that the 5% difference is subjectively large. While the individual does not explicitly mention 'skewness', they are attending to the distribution of probability over outcomes and making a judgment based on which lottery gives a better chance of winning something. This aligns with the core idea behind the skewness reason—favoring lotteries with higher probability on favorable outcomes—even if not framed in statistical terms. The justification (probability difference outweighs monetary difference) is consistent with a preference for more left-skewed distributions. Although the term 'skewness' is not used, the cognitive process described—evaluating which lottery gives a better chance at a win and prioritizing that over prize size—matches the functional logic of the decision reason. Therefore, it is highly likely the individual used a reasoning process equivalent to the skewness reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 90
